\f1 Note: This document generally follows the conventions of the \ul\i P?S/8\par
Keyboard Monitor Command Guide\ulnone\i0  available separately.\par
\par
\f2 P?S/8 supports an ASCII text paper-tape utility named \i\f0 P\f1 TUTIL\i0 .  As of P?S/8 Version 8Z, the current release is PTUTIL Version 1B.\par
\par
Note: PTUTIL is being introduced as an add-on to existing copies of\par
P?S/8; as such, it may not be present on any particular system.  Appendix A contains information on using PTUTIL on P?S/8 systems where PTUTIL is not currently installed in the System Programs Directory.\par
\par
PTUTIL is used to process 8-level punched paper-tapes in standard ASCII text format.  Note: Binary paper-tapes punched in standard BIN or RIM format are handled separately by the P?S/8 \i BIN\i0  program documented elsewhere.\par
\par
PTUTIL supports the standard high-speed reader/punch hardware [device 01/02] and console devices [03/04] that support paper-tape reader and punch capabilities such as Teletype Model 33 Automatic Send and Receive [ASR] and Model 35 ASR as modified to DEC specifications.  The choice of input [or output] device is determined by command-line option switches.\par
\par
If the source of the paper-tape input data is the low-speed ASR device, special handling of certain control characters such as \highlight1\f2 Control\f1\fs29 -\f2\fs24 C\highlight0\f1 , \highlight1\f2 Control\f1\fs29 -\f2\fs24 S\highlight0\f1 , \highlight1\f2 Control\f1\fs29 -\f2\fs24 Q\highlight0\f1  and \highlight1\f2 Control\f1\fs29 -\f2\fs24 O\highlight0\f1  will be disabled to prevent data garbling.  As such, only devices that do not require reverse flow control can be supported.  While this will always be the case on the supported Teletype models, PTUTIL may not be able to handle certain hybrid console devices that were never supported by DEC.\par
\par
If any reader compatibility issues arise [such as the need for flow control, which the supported devices do not use], PTUTIL must be used in an operating mode that does not echo or embellish the input on the console output device; command-line options are available to produce a variety of console output displays during reader input.  If necessary, all such options must be turned off.  Additionally, if any of the options that echo input data on the console output device are in effect, it will not be possible to detect \highlight1\f2 Control\f1\fs29 -\f2\fs24 O\highlight0\f1  [which flips the state of character echo on and off].  Program abort by the detection of \highlight1\f2 Control\f1\fs29 -\f2\fs24 C\highlight0\f1  will not be possible until reader timeout has occurred.\page Note: None of the restrictions stated above apply to paper-tape input from the high-speed reader; however, reader throughput can be degraded by echoing the characters on low-speed console output devices; the display speed must be of sufficient speed to maintain reader throughput of 300 characters/second [or perhaps only slightly less].\par
Most DEC high-speed readers must maintain a minimum throughput speed to avoid slowing down to a small fraction of normal operating speed. This situation can easily become further degraded by poor maintenance practice; many high-speed readers are misadjusted with respect to the speed threshold adjustment as most applications do not exhibit the problem.  Generally, all such difficulties can be prevented by avoiding input echoing to allow the reader to run within the higher-speed performance range.\par
\par
1.\tab ASCII text paper-tape format.\par
\par
A fully conforming ASCII text paper-tape consists of the following components:\par
\par
a)\tab Leader Section.\par
\par
\tab The leader section consists of one or more frames with only the \tab high-order bit punched.  Many paper-tape system programs tend to \tab create fairly lengthy leader sections to assist the operator in \tab placing the paper-tape in the reader.\par
\par
\tab When PTUTIL is used to create ASCII text paper-tapes, the default \tab leader length is 100 [octal 144] frames.  This value can be \tab overriden with the \f2 =\i xxxx\i0\f1  command-line parameter to produce a \tab longer or shorter leader [and trailer] section as desired.\par
\par
\tab Note: If the command-line includes the =0 parameter value, all \tab leader and trailer frames will be eliminated.  Setting the \tab parameter to =1023 [1777 octal] will produce the largest \tab leader/trailer section allowed; attempting to use a larger value \tab will yield an error message.\par
\par
\tab PTUTIL ignores the entire leader section when creating P?S/8 \tab output files from paper-tape input.\par
\par
b)\tab Data Section.\par
\par
\tab The data section generally consists of frames punched \tab with \tab [nearly] any ASCII value permitted.  While the parity bit will \tab be ignored, the specific combination of only punching the high-\tab order bit is not allowed in the data section to avoid \tab confusion \tab with leader or trailer frames.\par
\par
\tab Most paper-tape operating system programs create paper-tape \tab frames with the high-order bit set [which is known as mark \tab parity].  This is primarily done to allow visual distinction from \tab binary paper-tapes which generally do not punch the high-order \tab bit in the binary data section.\par
\par
\tab When PTUTIL is used to create ASCII text paper-tapes, all frames \tab in the data section will have the high-order bit set by default.  \tab The \tab disposition of the high-order bit in the data section may be \tab subject to PTUTIL command-line option switches that affect frame \tab parity.  All four frame parity variants are available.  When \tab reading paper-tapes, PTUTIL can optionally check the parity in \tab effect; program action when an error is detected is dependent on \tab other command-line options set.\par
\par
\tab When creating P?S/8 output files, the parity bit of all frames in \tab the data section is ignored.  All frames in the data section \tab are subject to conversion to P?S/8 SIXBIT text format as \tab described in Appendix C of the \ul\i P?S/8 Keyboard Monitor Command\par
\ulnone\i0\tab\ul\i Guide\ulnone\i0  available as a separate document.\par
\par
\tab Note: At the time of this writing, the document referenced above \tab is being updated to describe changes to recent releases of the \tab prevailing inter-system conversion programs including PTUTIL.  \tab The only issues under review concern the disposition of the \tab seldom used \highlight1\f2 @\highlight0\f1  and \highlight1\f2 _\highlight0\f1  characters; in previous releases of \i OS8CON \tab\i0 and related conversion programs, these characters were ignored. \tab In PTUTIL and future releases of the other conversion programs, \tab the characters will be converted to \highlight1\f2 #\highlight0\f1  and \highlight1\f2 -\highlight0\f1  respectively.\par
\par
c)\tab Trailer Section.\par
\par
\tab The trailer section consists of one or more frames with only the \tab high-order bit punched.  Many paper-tape system programs tend to \tab create fairly lengthy trailer sections to assist the operator in \tab placing the paper-tape in the reader.\par
\par
\tab Note: In some instances, there are multiple data sections within \tab a single paper-tape; the trailer of one section also serves as \tab the leader of the next section, etc.  PTUTIL supports command-\tab line options to manage multiple input sections or multiple tapes \tab as required.\par
\par
\tab When PTUTIL is used to create ASCII text paper-tapes, the default \tab trailer length is 100 [octal 144] frames.  This value can be \tab overriden with the \f2 =\i xxxx\i0\f1  command-line parameter to produce a \tab longer or shorter trailer [and leader] section as desired.  The \tab command-line switches apply equally to leader and trailer \tab lengths.\par
\par
\tab Note: When creating P?S/8 output files, PTUTIL closes the current \tab file on the first occurrence of a trailer character; all trailer\par
\tab characters are otherwise ignored.  The output file will also be \tab closed if there is no further paper-tape input as determined by \tab paper-tape reader device timeout.  As such, \tab the trailer section \tab is considered optional.  If multiple-tape options are in effect,\par
\tab the reader can be restarted as needed; the operator must\par
\tab reposition [or change] the tape in the reader device to an \tab appropriate leader or data section to allow further input during \tab the overall conversion session.\page d)\tab Sentinel Character.\par
\par
\tab A final frame may be punched with the \highlight1\f2 Control\f1\fs29 -\f2\fs24 Z\highlight0\f1  character code \tab with the high-order bit clear.  The sentinel character is \tab provided for compatibility with other storage-based operating \tab systems supporting this convention.  PTUTIL can create paper-\tab tapes with the sentinel character suppressed by using the '/Z' \tab command-line option.  It is recommended that the \highlight1\f2 Control\f1\fs29 -\f2\fs24 Z\highlight0\f1  \tab character be used in conjunction with a shortened trailer section \tab [or perhaps no trailer section].  Systems that support this \tab convention generally expect the sentinel character [or will \tab modify the trailer to conform to system standards].  [This method \tab is used in the TOPS-10 and OS/8 operating systems.  Other systems \tab hosting the SIMH simulator may have additional requirements.]\par
\par
2.   P?S/8 keyboard monitor commands to invoke PTUTIL.\par
\par
Command-lines are formed within P?S/8 allowing options to be passed to system programs in the same overall manner as OS/8 CCL commands [with a few minor variations and several significant extensions].\par
\par
Note: P?S/8 supports several command prompt variations.  The prompt used throughout this document is identical to that used in TOPS-10 and OS/8 to facilitate comparisons between the various operating systems.  \cf2\highlight3 <SP>\cf0\highlight0  and \cf2\highlight3 <HT>\cf0\highlight0  characters are freely allowed between command elements to enhance command readability and are otherwise ignored.\par
\par
Typical command examples to invoke PTUTIL [in slightly simplified form] are structured as follows:\par
\par
a)\tab Create P?S/8 output files from paper-tape input.\par
\par
\tab .[optional R or RU\{n\}] PTUTIL \cf2\highlight3 output files\cf0\highlight0  < \cf2\highlight3 option switches\cf0\highlight0\par
\i\par
\tab or\i0\par
\par
\tab .[optional R or RU\{n\}] PTUTIL > \cf2\highlight3 output files\cf0\highlight0\i  \cf2\highlight3\i0 option switches\cf0\highlight0  \par
\par
b)\tab Create paper-tapes from P?S/8 input files.\par
\par
\tab .[optional R or RU\{n\}] PTUTIL < \cf2\highlight3 input files\cf0\highlight0  \cf2\highlight3 option switches\cf0\highlight0\par
\i\par
\tab or\i0\par
\par
\tab .[optional R or RU\{n\}] PTUTIL \cf2\highlight3 input files\cf0\highlight0  >\i  \cf2\highlight3\i0 option switches\cf0\highlight0\par
\par
Note: PTUTIL must not be passed mixed input and output files.\par
\par
Note: Within keyboard monitor execution commands not specifying output files, the use of \highlight1\f2 <\highlight0\f1  and \highlight1\f2 >\highlight0\f1  characters is optional; they are often used to clarify command variations by delineating command sections while maintaining command symmetry.  The use of these characters is recommended within P?S/8 \i BATCH\i0  files to enhance readability.\page Note: The P?S/8 keyboard monitor allows command formation in either file specification order as shown above; this allows the user to work in a familiar environment regardless of prior experience [with various other DEC or similar operating systems].  The commands are generally similar to the various concise command implementations found in other DEC operating systems.\par
\par
Command-line option switches [and the \i =xxxx\i0  parameter if required] can be placed anywhere in the command line after the program name [in this instance \i\f0 P\f1 TUTIL\i0 ] and may be stated redundantly.\par
\par
For more information about P?S/8 command-line variations, consult the \ul\i P?S/8 Keyboard Monitor Command Guide\ulnone\i0  available as a separate document.\par
\par
3.\tab P?S/8 file formats supported by PTUTIL.\line\line PTUTIL supports both P?S/8 ASCII text file formats:\par
\par
a)\tab Fixed-length files that are compatible with the Tiny File System \tab [\i TFS\i0 ] directory,\par
\par
\tab\i and\i0\par
\par
b)\tab Extended-length files.  Note: Extended-length files are generally \tab available only as a single file on each logical device unit.\par
\par
TFS file support in PTUTIL.\par
\par
TFS files are supported directly by the P?S/8 Keyboard Monitor.  Each file is 2048 12-bit words.  In general, each file will tend to contain up to 3/4 of this capacity as six-bit bytes in the P?S/8 SIXBIT text format as described in Appendix C of the \ul\i P?S/8 Keyboard Monitor Command Guide\ulnone\i0  available as a separate document.  This corresponds to about 3000 characters per TFS file depending on average line length and the attendant line number overhead.\par
\par
Note: Each line in the file contains a pointer to the start of the line and a permanent line number.  This allows rapid editing of each file by the P?S/8 Keyboard Monitor which includes a 2048 word edit buffer to hold the entire file while performing any editing operation.  Changes are not permanent until the file is written back to the storage device.\par
\par
When PTUTIL is used to create TFS output files, each file includes automatically generated line numbers starting at 100 and incrementing by 10 for each additional line.  Files are closed when a \cf2\highlight3 <FF>\cf0\highlight0  character from the paper-tape is being processed and there is less than a reasonable amount of space for future editing left in the file.  Should there be insufficient space to store the latest line obtained from the paper-tape input, the latest file will be closed and the next file will be opened.  The process continues until the entire paper-tape is processed [or there is an error caused by insufficient output files passed to PTUTIL].\page PTUTIL can be passed up to 17 TFS output files which corresponds to about 50,000 text characters on the paper-tape input of any one session.  [Note: While this is generally considered a rather large quantity of input for practical use of ASCII text paper-tapes, P?S/8 easily handles far larger text projects if necessary.  This would generally be accomplished by additional processing as documented within the \ul\i P?S/8 Keyboard Monitor Command Guide\ulnone\i0  and is beyond the scope of this document.]  An error message will be issued if insufficient output files are supplied in the command invoking PTUTIL.  As such, it is recommended to supply more output files than contemplated; PTUTIL supplies a summary of how many output files were actually used; the rest of the files [if any] can be repurposed.\par
\par
Extended-length file support in PTUTIL.\par
\par
PTUTIL also supports P?S/8 extended-length ASCII text output files.  This file format is identical to the TFS file format in terms of text characters; however, no line numbers or related information exists.  As such, extended-length output files can be created up to the storage limit on each logical device unit selected for the file creation.\par
\par
Appendix B describes the current and future implementation of extended-length files as it applies to P?S/8 Version 8Z and future releases.  As a practical matter, there can only be as many as eight extended-length output files where each file is placed on a different logical device unit.  [More modest systems may support less than the maximum of eight logical units limiting this facility still further].\par
\par
Extended-length output files are closed when the latest line of paper-tape input cannot be processed in the last output block available on the current logical unit.  An error message will be issued if there are insufficient extended-length output files.  PTUTIL supplies a summary of how many extended-length output files were actually used during paper-tape processing; the rest of the files can be repurposed.\par
\par
When PTUTIL is used to create ASCII text paper-tape, all line number information [if any] is ignored.  As such, TFS and extended-length input files can be freely mixed.  PTUTIL can be passed up to 17 input files.  On sufficiently large P?S/8 configurations, this corresponds to as much as 8,000,000 paper-tape output frames [not counting leader and trailer].  [Note: For all paper-tape frame tallies and summaries, PTUTIL calculates the various paper-tape frame counts in 24-bit unsigned integer arithmetic.  While these numbers may appear to be preposterously large, one of the primary purposes of PTUTIL is to interface P?S/8 to external host files when used in a simulated environment such as the SIMH PDP-8 simulator package.  All inter-system file interchange is via paper-tape simulation.  For the very largest practical PDP-8 programs ever written, this may not be enough for any one project.  However, such programs tax all PDP-8 development systems whether on real hardware or via simulation.  In many ways,\par
P?S?8 can provided superior support for these projects for at least the development phase; generally these programs are written as stand-alone bootable applications as no PDP-8 development system is adequate for running them.]\page 4.\tab Command-line option switch and passed numeric value format.\par
\par
The P?S/8 command-line supports option-switches passed in a manner consistent with many DEC operating systems [such as \i OS/8\i0  \i CCL\i0 ].  Two variant forms are supported:\par
\par
a)\tab Individual option switches each preceded by \cf2\highlight3 /\cf0\highlight0  such as:\par
\par
\tab /F /U /V\par
\par
b)\tab Grouped switchs preceded by \cf2\highlight3 (\cf0\highlight0  such as:\par
\par
\tab (FUV)\par
\par
The trailing \cf2\highlight3 )\cf0\highlight0  is optional unless needed to delimit the option group from another command section.\par
\par
A numeric value can be passed to system programs using the =\i xxxx\i0  syntax where \i xxxx\i0  is any value of up to four digits in the range of 0 through 4094 [or 0000 through 7776 octal].  [Note: The explicit value of 4095 [or 7777 octal] is not allowed; however, that value is passed to the program to indicate the explicit form of =\i xxxx\i0  was not used in the command.]\par
\par
4.\tab Using PTUTIL to punch paper-tapes from P?S/8 input files.\par
\par
When P?S/8 input files are passed to PTUTIL, paper-tape output data is created.  Up to 17 [21 octal] P?S/8 input files can be passed to PTUTIL; the output will be a continuous stream of paper-tape frames taken from the concatenated contents of all input files.  Note: Extended-length input files may be freely used with TFS files in this operating mode.\par
\par
The following command-line options apply:\par
\par
\tab /A\tab Set automatic mode.  All prompts to the user are eliminated.  \tab\tab The paper-tape punch hardware must be ready before running \tab\tab\tab PTUTIL.  Automatic mode can be used to create fully \tab\tab\tab\tab automated P?S/8 BATCH sessions for repetitive tasks.\par
\par
\tab /F\tab The default paper-tape output device is the optional low-\tab\tab\tab speed punch of the Teletype model 33 or model 35 console \tab\tab\tab device.  If the '/F' command-line option switch is set, the \tab\tab output device is changed to the DEC standard high-speed \tab\tab\tab punch [device 02].\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: High-speed punch support is subject to time-out.  If \tab\tab\tab the high-speed punch is not available, the output will be \tab\tab\tab redirected to the console device.  Unless the '/S' command-\tab\tab\tab line option switch is set [silent mode], a console message \tab\tab\tab will be issued if the output is redirected.\page\tab /S\tab If the '/S' command-line option switch is set, all console \tab\tab\tab output is suppressed [silent-running mode] with the \tab\tab\tab\tab exception of important error messages.  Silent-running mode \tab\tab can be used in BATCH operations that might otherwise create \tab\tab unwanted console output.  [Note: P?S/8 BATCH can suppress \tab\tab\tab its own console output.  A carefully crafted BATCH file \tab\tab\tab could punch a series of paper-tapes without creating \tab\tab\tab\tab unwanted message strings that could be punched on the tape.] \par
\par
\tab /U\tab As necessary, various console messages will be issued by \tab\tab\tab PTUTIL.  By default, all messages are printed in mixed-case \tab\tab characters.  If the '/U' command-line option switch is set, \tab\tab all messages will be printed in upper-case characters.\par
\par
  \tab\tab Note: Certain console terminal devices automatically "fold" \tab\tab lower-case characters to upper-case; this option is only \tab\tab\tab required if the console terminal does not support any form \tab\tab\tab of direct lower-case printing capability.\par
\par
