\par
Output File Options.\par
\par
If explicit binary output files are not given, the following option switches can be used:\par
\par
\tab /B\tab The \cf2\highlight3 %\cf0\highlight0  file on the P?S/8 system device bootup logical unit \tab\tab\tab [see description of the /U option switch below] is used as \tab\tab\tab the [first] binary output file.\par
 \par
\tab /D\tab If the /B option switch is set, the \cf2\highlight3 $\cf0\highlight0  on the P?S/8 system \tab\tab\tab device bootup logical unit [see description of the /U \tab\tab\tab\tab option switch below] is used as the second binary output \tab\tab\tab file [if required].\page\tab /U\tab Change the logical unit that applies [when the /B and /D \tab\tab\tab option switches are set as described above] from the system \tab\tab device bootup logical unit to the result of the following \tab\tab\tab calculation: \{System device bootup logical unit\} \cf2\highlight3 XOR\cf0\highlight0  1.  \tab\tab\tab This selects the "opposite" logical unit of the primary pair \tab\tab of logical drive units.  [Note: Certain P?S/8 systems can be \tab\tab booted to non-zero logical units; as such, this \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab implementation will generally allow a useful drive pairing.]\par
\par
\tab Note: If the second binary output file is not required, the /D \tab option switch will effectively be ignored; assembly statistics \tab indicate the actual output file count.\par
\par
\tab Due to their physical proximity to the beginning of the storage \tab device, specifying the \cf2\highlight3 %\cf0\highlight0  and \cf2\highlight3 $\cf0\highlight0  files can greatly speed up various \tab system operations.  Additionally, these files are not subject to \tab the overhead of TFS directory operations with regard to the \tab TFS directory on the same logical device unit; as such, many \tab operations have significantly lower overhead when these files are \tab used strategically.  On tape-based systems, \tab throughput can be \tab further improved by using a logical unit different from the \tab system bootup logical unit.\par
\par
\tab Note: The /B, /D and /U option switches are provided to simplify \tab the process of allocating output files as most assemblies require \tab one or two output files.  All file specifications can designate \tab an optional device unit; as such, any combination of files can \tab explicitly state associated logical device units as necessary.  \tab The use of these option switches merely allows the use of the \tab more commonly used output file combinations stated in a more \tab succinct form.\par
\par
\tab If more than two binary output files are required, or the created \tab binary files should be written to permanent TFS file names, the \tab command line must include explicitly stated output files.\par
\par
Chaining Options.\par
\par
\tab If desired, automatic chaining options can be invoked to further \tab process output files created during the assembly:\par
\par
\tab\f0 /G\f1\tab\f0 The output files created during the assembly are passed as \f1\tab\tab\tab\f0 input files to the\f1  \f0 P?S/8 BIN utility for loading\f1  and \tab\tab\tab\tab execution\f0  [or\f1  punching binary paper-tapes as described \tab\tab\tab below\f0 ]\f1 ; a\f0 dditional\f1  \f0 loading \f1 [or punching] \f0 option\f1 s\f0  may\f1  be\f0  \f1\tab\tab\tab applied.  Note: Chaining to P?S/8 BIN is prevented if there \tab\tab are any assembly errors.\page\tab\tab When chaining to P?S/8 BIN for the purpose of loading binary \tab\tab files, see the section of this document pertaining to \tab\tab\tab\tab additional binary chaining options for information \tab\tab\tab\tab regarding the /H, /I, /V and /Z option switches and the \tab\tab\tab\f0 =\i\f1 xxxx\i0  passed numerical value [possibly in conjunction with \tab\tab\tab the /1 through /7 option switches].  Note: The /W option \tab\tab\tab switch must be clear when loading binary files.\par
\par
\tab\tab When chaining to P?S/8 BIN for the purpose of punching \tab\tab\tab binary paper-tape output, see the section of this document \tab\tab\tab pertaining to additional binary chaining options for \tab\tab\tab\tab information regarding the /F, /R and /W option switches.  \tab\tab\tab Note: The /W option switch must be set when punching binary \tab\tab paper-tape output.\par
\par
\tab /M\tab The output files created during the assembly are passed as \tab\tab\tab input files to the P?S/8 \i MAP\i0  utility to create a\tab  bitmap of \tab\tab the assembled program indicating actual memory usage.\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: The format of P?S/8 MAP bitmap output is generally \tab\tab\tab consistent with the output produced by PAL10 after any FIELD \tab\tab directive is used [or end of assembly] and is a superset of \tab\tab the output of OS/8 \i BITMAP\i0 .  The title format and pagination \tab\tab specifics are the same as those used for the assembler \tab\tab\tab listing output [if any] and assembly statistics pages [which \tab\tab in part depend on the usage of the /P option switch \tab\tab\tab\tab described below].\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: When the P?S/8 MAP utility is used without chaining, \tab\tab\tab there are options available to limit which memory fields are \tab\tab subject to mapping; during the chaining process from P?S/8 \tab\tab\tab PAL, these options are inhibited.  As such, all memory \tab\tab\tab fields are elegible for bitmap output following the \tab\tab\tab\tab assembly; however, the output will be limited to fields \tab\tab\tab actually referenced during the assembly.\par
\par
\tab\tab It is possible to specify chaining options to P?S/8 MAP and\par
\tab\tab P?S/8 BIN in a single command; the chain to P?S/8 MAP will \tab\tab\tab always be performed first.  [Note: The further chain to\par
\tab\tab P?S/8 BIN will be prevented if assembly errors occurred.]\par
\par
Pass Control Options.\par
\par
\tab /K\tab Perform a two-pass assembly; the default is a single \tab\tab\tab\tab assembly pass.  Note: The /K option switch is not required \tab\tab\tab if any binary or listing options are specified, as these \tab\tab\tab functions intrinsically require two pass operation.\par
\par
\tab\tab Unlike OS/8 PAL8 which always performs two [or three] \tab\tab\tab\tab passes, P?S/8 PAL can perform a useful single-pass assembly \tab\tab [which is consistent with the paper-tape system assembler \tab\tab\tab program known as PAL III].\page\tab\tab At the end of pass one, all symbols still not defined are \tab\tab\tab shown on the system console in sorted order; the value \tab\tab\tab indicated is the address within the assembly where each \tab\tab\tab undefined symbol was first encountered.  This facilitates \tab\tab\tab early program development when it is likely that \tab portions \tab\tab\tab of the program have not yet been written.  Alternatively,\par
\tab\tab variables and temporaries may not yet defined [or perhaps \tab\tab\tab are the object of typographical errors which can cause \tab\tab\tab problems when\tab certain symbols have names similar to other \tab\tab\tab symbols]; any problem of this nature can generally be \tab\tab\tab\tab quickly resolved by merely referencing the relatively short \tab\tab list of undefined symbols.\par
\par
\tab\tab Contrast this functionality with that provided by TOPS-10 \tab\tab\tab PAL10 or OS/8 PAL8, neither of which support a mechanism to \tab\tab highlight undefined symbols.  The only \tab facility provided in \tab\tab these assembly programs is to create a second pass [or third \tab\tab pass] listing file of the entire assembly; searching through \tab\tab this far more voluminous output can be tedious as there are \tab\tab usually several references to each undefined symbol as well \tab\tab as many lines of output unrelated to the particular errors.\par
\tab\tab [Note: In perverse instances, large quantities of extraneous \tab\tab output could be triggered by the failure to correctly define \tab\tab key symbols during early program development; these \tab\tab\tab\tab circumstances could make the manual process of eliminating \tab\tab\tab errors far worse due to cascade dependencies.]\par
\par
\par
\par
\tab\tab As such, using OS/8 PAL8 can easily become a time-consuming \tab\tab process.  In such circumstances, it would be prudent to \tab\tab\tab limit the assembly to a single pass using P?S/8 PAL until \tab\tab\tab key problems are solved.  Using only the undefined symbol \tab\tab\tab report available at the end of pass one of P?S/8 PAL, most \tab\tab\tab problematic cases can generally be eliminated.  [Note: A two \tab\tab pass listing provided by P?S/8 PAL would be as frustrating\tab\tab\tab to use as that obtained from OS/8 PAL8; however, only P?S/8 \tab\tab PAL provides the one pass method that is recommended \tab for \tab\tab\tab early program development.]\par
\par
\tab\tab [Note: See the description of the /L and /N option switches \tab\tab for the alternate usage of the /K option switch.]\par
\par
\f0 Second \f1 pass \f0 assembly considerations.\par
\par
\f1\tab A second assembly pass is performed if binary output files are \tab created and/or assembly listing output is produced.  During pass \tab two, any additional error messages will be displayed on the \tab prevailing output device [system console or line printer] in a \tab format similar to that of OS/8 PAL8 and TOPS-10 PAL10 [including \tab the nearest symbol plus any numerical \tab offset] to facilitate \tab source code development; additional information may be displayed \tab in certain instances.\par
\par
\tab Programmers may embellish the displayed output of the assembly \tab process when critical sections are being processed:\par
\par
\tab a)\tab P?S/8 PAL supports the ERROR directive; this causes a \tab\tab\tab\tab deliberate assembly error.  By using the ERROR directive, \tab\tab\tab the programmer can design internal safeguards to prevent \tab\tab\tab logical inconsistencies that might occur during program \tab\tab\tab maintenance.  [Note: The design of safeguarded code sections \tab\tab generally includes the use of conditional assembly \tab\tab\tab\tab statements to calculate problematic scenarios; as long as \tab\tab\tab certain specific requirements are met, there will be no \tab\tab\tab output of the ERROR directive.]\page\tab\tab Note: The output of the ERROR directive is consistent with\tab\tab\tab that of all standard errors during each assembly pass and \tab\tab\tab can include the display of an optional evaluated argument. \tab\tab\tab Automatic binary loading by chaining to P?S/8 BIN will be \tab\tab\tab aborted if for no other reason than the ERROR directive was \tab\tab invoked.\par
\par
\tab b)\tab P?S/8 PAL supports the PAUSE directive both as it was \tab\tab\tab\tab originally implemented in PAL III [to ignore the rest of the \tab\tab contents of the latest input file] and also as an extension \tab\tab which can embellish displayed output during the assembly \tab\tab\tab process.\par
\par
\tab\tab When the PAUSE directive is used with an argument, the \tab\tab\tab argument is evaluated as a 12-bit unsigned value.  Displayed \tab\tab output is consistent with the format of standard error \tab\tab\tab messages; however, PAUSE usage does not report as an error.\par
\par
\tab\tab The programmer can use the PAUSE directive for a variety of \tab\tab purposes [such as announcing the assembly pass or start of \tab\tab\tab assembly of a critical section].  When a logical \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab inconsistency is detected, the PAUSE directive can be used \tab\tab\tab to output additional information about the error to help \tab\tab\tab maintainence programmers understand the nature of the\tab\tab\tab\tab problem.\par
\par
\tab\tab The techniques described above and other features unique to \tab\tab P?S/8 PAL can be qualified to prevent use with incompatible \tab\tab assemblers.\par
\par
\tab\tab The PQS directive is defined only in P?S/8 PAL; this allows \tab\tab the programmer to create conditional assembly statements to \tab\tab restrict usage of these features to P?S/8 PAL and not \tab\tab\tab\tab generate erroneous errors caused by the failure to support \tab\tab\tab the features in other assemblers.  An example of this usage \tab\tab follows:\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab IFNDEF\tab PQS\tab <PQS= 0>\tab /WILL BE ZERO IF NOT P?S PAL.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab IFNZRO\tab PQS\tab <PAUSE .>\tab /DISPLAY CURRENT ORIGIN.\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: The XLIST directive, as implemented in P?S/8 PAL, is \tab\tab\tab consistent with OS/8 PAL8; XLIST statements can be used to \tab\tab\tab hide portions of the assembly as necessary such as \tab\tab\tab\tab assembler-specific statements.  Deployed judiciously, all \tab\tab\tab unassembled conditional assembly can be hidden when \tab\tab\tab\tab producing the listing output.\page Symbol Table Options.\par
\par
\tab /S\tab The symbol table will be output at the end of the assembly.\par
\par
\tab\tab If the /N option switch is also set, the symbol table format \tab\tab will be consistent with that of an enhanced listing pass [if \tab\tab enabled].\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: The exact output may be subject to the number of \tab\tab\tab assembly passes performed; two pass assembly is performed if \tab\tab binary output files are created and/or listing output is \tab\tab\tab produced, or the /K option switch is set.\par
\par
\tab /A\tab If the /S option switch is set, the entire symbol table will \tab\tab be output at the end of the assembly; this includes all \tab\tab\tab permanent symbols and assembly directives.\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: If dual-mode [PDP-8 and LINC] assembly is in effect, \tab\tab\tab the symbol table will be set to that which applies at the \tab\tab\tab end of the assembly; certain symbols with dual definitions \tab\tab\tab will display values as used during the final assembly mode.\par
\par
\tab\tab Certain permanent symbols may have been redefined by the \tab\tab\tab source code; this is especially important in LINC mode \tab\tab\tab assembly where there may be multiple definitions of certain \tab\tab instructions that are model-dependent within the various \tab\tab\tab LINC implementations.\par
\par
\tab\tab While all default LMODE definitions within P?S/8 PAL are for \tab\tab the PDP-12 LINC variant, source code changes can be made to\tab\tab\tab redefine the affected symbols for compatibility with the \tab\tab\tab LINC-8 or classic LINC as required.  [Note: Permanent \tab\tab\tab\tab changes of certain SKP class or other LINC instructions can \tab\tab only be made at the source-code level of P?S/8 PAL.  The \tab\tab\tab best overall recommendation is to redefine the affected \tab\tab\tab symbols to be consistent with the target architectural \tab\tab\tab model.]\par
\par
Listing Options.\par
\par
\tab /L\tab Assembly listing output will be produced.\par
\par
\tab\tab Unlike OS/8 PAL8, P?S/8 PAL performs at most two passes.\par
\page\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\f2\par
Appendix A.\par
\par
Using PTUTIL on P?S/8 systems without PTUTIL installed.\par
\par
PTUTIL is a recent addition to releases of P?S/8 Version 8Z; it will be included in all future releases as a standard system program compatible with the concise or extended forms of program execution.\par
\par
Those familiar with the P?S/8 system programs directory can add PTUTIL as long as there is sufficient free storage space.  Failing this, it is also possible to run PTUTIL as an impromptu system program as described below; the binary file[s] created by assembling PTUTIL source file[s] must be available on an available logical device unit [drive 1 in this example].\par
\par
