\par
PTUTIL is designed to be compatible with the work-around mechanism described above to allow rapid addition to existing systems; this mechanism was introduced in P?S/8 Version 8Y and is supported on all newer releases.\par
\par
Usage restrictions.\par
\par
When using PTUTIL to create ASCII text paper-tape output, the default leader/trailer length [surrounding the characters obtained from \par
P?S/8 input files] is 100 [144 octal] frames.  This parameter can be changed using the =\i xxxx\i0  parameter which is normally expressed in the current keyboard monitor radix [which is generally decimal].  However,  this execution attribute is only available to programs located in the system programs directory [the default value is to force octal radix].  As such, any usage of the =\i xxxx\i0  parameter as described here must be expressed in octal.\par
\par
End of Appendix A.\page\f1\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\f2\par
Appendix B.\par
\par
Extended-length file support in P?S/8 Version 8Z and future releases.\par
\par
The notion of extended-length files has been well-established in P?S/8 over the years including the file format for ASCII text files as used in PTUTIL and other inter-system conversion utilities [\i OS8CON\i0 , \i L6DCON\i0 , etc.].  However, the directory structure to manage extended-length files has gone through several incomplete implementations, all of which are being scrapped in favor of the P?S/8 SHELL which is expected to be released as a part of P?S/8 Version 9B.  [Version 9A will be a cleanup release to clear the backlog of lesser issues that have surfaced over the years that require slightly changing many programs to achieve various improvements.  Despite this, very little will be changed from the vantage point of the end user or P?S/8 developer with the exception of how to access extended-length files.]\par
\par
As of this writing, P?S/8 Version 8Z contains a vestigial structure that provides the rudiments of supporting extended-length files with several provisos.\par
\par
a)\tab The user doesn't actually supply extended-length file \tab specifications.  Instead, the user supplies an existing TFS file \tab name which must be the highest file [by storage block] placed \tab into the TFS directory [and would be the last file name shown by \tab the CA\{TALOG\} command].  Clearly this implementation is quite \tab flawed as the \tab user must be aware of the restriction for which \tab there are no safeguards.  All files physically further up the \tab device beyond the file name specified are subject to destruction.\par
\par
b)\tab The structure known as the extended-length directory is used for \tab only one purpose: To establish the highest block on the current \tab logical unit to limit the size of an extended-length file.\par
\par
c)\tab There is no facility to name extended-length files [which is moot \tab since they are not referenced currently].\par
\par
d)\tab The rest of the extended-length directory structure is unused. \tab It was created only for a theoretical attempt at a multiple-file \tab directory structure.  This has since been scrapped in favor of \tab the P?S/8 SHELL which is currently under development.\par
\par
As such, programs such as PTUTIL check to see if the '/E' command-line switch is set, but cannot validate the file handles as part of the extended-length file system blocks.  [In fact, this is impossible presently;the passed blocks are merely the highest blocks currently used in the TFS directory; files will be created from that passed block up to as much as all the rest of the blocks on the storage device.]  Thus arises the user caveat to always pass the highest actual TFS file in the directory to avoid file destruction.]\page\cf3 Appendix B [continued].\par
\par
\cf0 All existing [relevant] conversion programs that create P?S/8 extended-length output files reserve internal initialization code space for future implementation where extended-length files are confirmed to start outside of the scope of the TFS directory and appropriate validation can be applied [including appropriate error messages].  When this is operational, the temporary kludge of supplying the starting block of the highest-block existing TFS file will cease to function; safe and proper support of the extended-length file will replace this risky situation.\cf3\par
\par
\cf0 The entire block structure of the extended-length file directory is being removed [and the blocks repurposed] from P?S/8 and will be replaced by a streamlined mechanism that formalizes the role of the extended-length file in a rational and completely safe manner.\line\line In future P?S/8 releases, it will be necessary to use the R or RU\{N\} form of program execution to access extended-length files.  This is in keeping with the original intention of P?S/8 extended-length file support; all proposed support mechanisms require sufficient code space in memory that cannot coexist with the keyboard monitor edit buffer.  \par
\par
[Note: The keyboard monitor provides support routines to the execution-class code sections that are brought into memory, including the concise execution mechanism that overlays a portion of the keyboard monitor.  This overall scheme is especially useful to certain utility programs that interact directly with the keyboard monitor in various ways.  Certain programs are designed to post-process the command line for additional information; a good example is the P?S/8 \i SET\i0  utility.  The entry for the SET program in the system programs directory indicates limited parsing by the execution-class command processor to allow SET to process commands via its own syntax parsing routines.]\par
\par
Historically, in all relevant implementations of P?S/8, a distinction has always been made between supporting the first physical half of the system programs directory [the first two blocks] and supporting the entire system programs directory due to specific resource issues.\par
\par
The concise form of program execution [where merely the program name is invoked causing it to be executed] restricts the entry for the program to be executed to the first half of the system programs directory.  This allows the P?S8 keyboard monitor to overlay the code that supports the execution and the first half of the system programs directory over a portion of the keyboard monitor [containing routines that are not used during the command-line processing].\page\cf3 Appendix B [continued].\par
\cf0\par
Note: Where relevant, the displaced portion of the keyboard monitor can be restored after the command is processed; this is important in case of programs such as the \i FIND\i0  and \i CHANGE\i0  utilities which interact with the keyboard monitor and the edit buffer.  Most program execution commands merely lead to loading the program image into some portion of user memory [locations 00000-07577] which then exit to location 07600 to reload the entire keyboard monitor; no interaction with the keyboard monitor is required.\par
\par
The advantage of this form of execution is the preservation of the edit buffer which is crucial to the execution of the overlay type of utilities [and P?S/8 \i BATCH\i0 ].  When PTUTIL is used without extended-length files, this is a suitable means for invoking the utility.\par
\par
One reason for using the R [or RU\{N\}] form of execution is to allow system program names that are anomalous to keyboard monitor commands.  Additionally, since the second half of the system programs directory is accessible, this allows as many as 85 system programs instead of the concise execution limit of 42 system programs [that are stored in the first half of the system programs directory].\par
\par
All program entries contained in the second half of the systems program directory must of necessity not depend on the preservation of the edit buffer.  [Note: This would generally be the case considering how few programs actually require this support.  For most programs, the somewhat lower overhead of the concise form of execution is merely a welcome slight improvement in performance but not an absolute requirement.]\cf3\par
\par
\cf0 As a practical matter, no existent P?S/8 release has ever fully filled the first half of the system programs directory; the typical system program count is 28.  [This has partially been a result of ensuring all existing program names are not in conflict with the keyboard monitor commands; for most users, there aren't all that many programs that warrant placement in the system programs directory; most programs can be run as user binary files to be loaded by the BIN program's slurp loader.]  As such, there haven't been sufficient system programs to require the full length of the system programs directory.  [Future specific system releases could cause these restrictions to be more meaningful; diagnostic programs could be added to a designated P?S/8 system directory or other purpose.]\par
\cf3\par
\cf0 Long term, it was always planned to require these restrictions to access extended-length output files and the entire system programs directory by overlaying the edit buffer [and potentially requiring a system reload if various errors were detected in the command-line].  Until the solution described here is implemented, the workaround for P?S/8 Version 8Z described in Appendix A above must be used.\page\cf3 Appendix B [continued].\par
\cf0\par
Extended-length files will be formally redefined in future releases of P?S/8 starting with Version 9A.  Each logical unit will support one extended-length file; supported file formats will not change.  As a practical matter, the extended-length file starting block will be exactly 0020 octal blocks past the best-case work-around of supplying the highest possible TFS file name.\par
\par
[Note: By design, the extended-length file storage follows the highest file permitted in the TFS directory.  The present workaround actually allows 0020 octal additional blocks for the largest possible extended-length file as compared to the eventual formal implementation.  However, the extent of the TFS directory can always be adjusted downward to allow more storage allocated to the extended-length file on the logical device.  If desired, the TFS directory can be rendered moot causing all available storage past the last block reserved by the system programs directory to be available for the extended-length file.  P?S/8 storage devices such as DECtape could be deliberately configured this way for routine use of extended-length files only.]\par
\par
The actual implementation of this support is to repurpose a few words at the end of the system programs directory for the extended-length file parameters.  This will have minimal effect on the system directory capacity; in the worst case implemenation, two less entries  will be available for additional system programs [leaving a maximum of 83 program entries].  As a practical matter, the system programs directory is presently utilized at a 40% level; as such, this will be of no particular consequence.]\line\line Since the extended length file entry will be located in the system programs directory, it will have already been read in with the rest of the system programs structure when the R [or RU\{N\}] form of execution is invoked.  Thus, there will be virtualy no additional overhead [actually somewhat less overhead] when specifying extended-length files once the full execution coding is revamped to support this in future releases.\par
\par
[Note: The extended-files directory structure blocks will be repurposed into part of the new low-level keyboard monitor used to manage all structures that overlay the keyboard monitor such as the Logical Console Overlay and the P?S/8 SHELL.  Additional blocks may be repurposed to other modules of the P?S/8 SHELL implementation such as the virtualized copy of the File and Communications area.]\par
\par
Once this revised form of program execution is fully implemented, all programs such as PTUTIL will be revised to fully support extended-length files with proper validation [meaning starting blocks will of necessity fall just outside of the TFS structure that precedes it].  In future releases, PTUTIL can check the TFS directory structure on the same logical unit the extended-length file entry passed resides.\par
The limit block of the TFS directory defines the lowest block allowed for extended-length files.\page\cf3 Appendix B [continued].\par
\cf0\par
Access to the dedicated entry in the system programs directory for the single extended-length file will require the user to set an appropriate file name that is unique and not anomalous to the TFS directory to avoid confusion.  If there is sufficient code space, the execution code can implement safeguards to ensure unique file names to enforce the restriction.  [PTUTIL could easily perform a lookup of the passed file name to ensure it is not present in both directories; this situation would lead to appropriate error messages during the initialization phase of PTUTIL startup coding.]\par
\par
File names for extended-length files will be implemented using SET commands to establish proper naming conventions as well as preventing file name anomaly.  Additional SET commands will manage scratch extended-length file lists for programs such as P?S/8 \i PAL\i0 , which requires long temporary files during the operation of the cross-reference module.  [Up to eight extended-length files can be made available for reference storage during the assembly.]\par
\par
End of Appendix B.\par
